00:10:44.963 --> 00:10:46.809
preschool at Stanford at 75 years.

00:10:46.809 --> 00:10:48.887
And now, it's launched with

00:10:48.887 --> 00:10:50.718
incredible philanthropy

00:10:50.718 --> 00:10:52.723
from John Doerr in a big way.

00:10:52.723 --> 00:10:54.729
As soon as that decision was made,

00:10:54.729 --> 00:10:56.526
there was a question of how will

00:10:56.526 --> 00:10:58.402
the other schools be involved?

00:10:58.402 --> 00:10:59.969
You need to have business involved

00:10:59.969 --> 00:11:01.269
if you're going to think about

00:11:01.269 --> 00:11:03.057
climate change, energy transition,

00:11:03.057 --> 00:11:04.519
innovation that's going to scale

00:11:04.519 --> 00:11:06.307
solutions, technical solutions that

00:11:06.307 --> 00:11:07.335
might come out of great

00:11:07.335 --> 00:11:08.598
engineering and science.

00:11:08.598 --> 00:11:09.756
And so we got started at the

00:11:09.756 --> 00:11:11.393
business school having discussions

00:11:11.393 --> 00:11:12.923
initially among the faculty, but

00:11:12.923 --> 00:11:14.467
then including lots more people.

00:11:14.467 --> 00:11:15.976
And we came up with a plan for

00:11:15.976 --> 00:11:17.885
an initiative that Bill Burnett,

00:11:17.885 --> 00:11:19.791
one of our faculty members has been

00:11:19.791 --> 00:11:20.405
leading.

00:11:20.405 --> 00:11:21.668
And now it's a joint

00:11:21.668 --> 00:11:23.902
initiative with the Doerr school.

00:11:23.902 --> 00:11:26.176
And so when the Doerr school opened

00:11:26.176 --> 00:11:28.372
in September, we had six faculty

00:11:28.372 --> 00:11:30.726
who took joint appointments over in

00:11:30.726 --> 00:11:31.992
the Doerr school.

00:11:31.992 --> 00:11:33.935
We have 70 MBA students who

00:11:33.935 --> 00:11:35.879
are doing a joint degree with

00:11:35.879 --> 00:11:37.183
the Doerr school.

00:11:37.183 --> 00:11:39.255
We have a whole set of classes that

00:11:39.255 --> 00:11:41.478
are getting introduced this year,

00:11:41.478 --> 00:11:42.886
we're running 17,

00:11:42.886 --> 00:11:44.452
this is actually crazy.

00:11:44.452 --> 00:11:46.249
I don't know why we're doing this

00:11:46.249 --> 00:11:47.675
scale, but we're running

00:11:47.675 --> 00:11:49.412
17 conferences in business and

00:11:49.412 --> 00:11:51.397
sustainability, one every two weeks

00:11:51.397 --> 00:11:52.586
all through the year.

00:11:52.586 --> 00:11:54.656
So climate finance, accounting,

00:11:54.656 --> 00:11:56.794
net zero operations, it's a little

00:11:56.794 --> 00:11:58.321
overwhelming actually.

00:11:58.321 --> 00:12:00.430
I think Anada is running one on PG

00:12:00.430 --> 00:12:02.462
and in the wild fires that I've

00:12:02.462 --> 00:12:04.573
gotta figure what I'm going to say

00:12:04.573 --> 00:12:06.525
at, but I will figure it out on

00:12:06.525 --> 00:12:08.029
that, don't worry.

00:12:08.029 --> 00:12:10.373
And then we're going to announce in

00:12:10.373 --> 00:12:11.879
a couple of weeks a new

00:12:11.879 --> 00:12:14.393
sustainability entrepreneurship

00:12:14.393 --> 00:12:15.817
program that we've

00:12:15.817 --> 00:12:18.426
put together with the Doerr school.

00:12:18.426 --> 00:12:20.770
I'm really excited in the kind of

00:12:20.770 --> 00:12:22.781
vision that we've had about

00:12:22.781 --> 00:12:24.133
the partnership.

00:12:24.133 --> 00:12:26.418
Is that this is an opportunity as

00:12:26.418 --> 00:12:28.367
we have with other Stanford

00:12:28.367 --> 00:12:30.740
schools to marry that incredible

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:33.367
disciplinary expertise in energy,

00:12:33.367 --> 00:12:36.077
and innovation, and technology, in

00:12:36.077 --> 00:12:38.534
the rest of campus with leadership

00:12:38.534 --> 00:12:40.907
entrepreneurship management of

00:12:40.907 --> 00:12:42.770
the business school, and

00:12:42.770 --> 00:12:45.503
that's just a dynamic combination.

00:12:45.503 --> 00:12:48.348
That's the combination that

00:12:48.348 --> 00:12:51.648
students need to be successful and

00:12:51.648 --> 00:12:53.593
to solve problems.

00:12:53.593 --> 00:12:55.902
You asked about the divestment from

00:12:55.902 --> 00:12:57.674
the sort of issues around,

00:12:57.674 --> 00:12:59.757
it was question about weather.

00:12:59.757 --> 00:13:02.084
So there's been a lot of debate at

00:13:02.084 --> 00:13:04.664
Stanford about forming the school,

00:13:04.664 --> 00:13:07.160
about whether Stanford should take

00:13:07.160 --> 00:13:09.670
money from fossil fuel companies.

00:13:09.670 --> 00:13:12.787
I would say opinions are divided at

00:13:12.787 --> 00:13:14.407
the University.

00:13:14.407 --> 00:13:15.952
There's a very vocal group

00:13:15.952 --> 00:13:17.698
of students and some alumni and

00:13:17.698 --> 00:13:19.715
faculty who feel very strongly that

00:13:19.715 --> 00:13:21.396
we shouldn't take money from

00:13:21.396 --> 00:13:22.554
energy companies.

00:13:22.554 --> 00:13:24.859
And then there's other people who

00:13:24.859 --> 00:13:26.608
have a different view that

00:13:26.608 --> 00:13:28.596
the university should engage

00:13:28.596 --> 00:13:30.981
broadly, and we have the principle

00:13:30.981 --> 00:13:33.050
of academic freedom which says

00:13:33.050 --> 00:13:35.356
faculty pretty much have the right

00:13:35.356 --> 00:13:37.662
to work on whatever they want, and

00:13:37.662 --> 00:13:39.651
that discussion is going to play

00:13:39.651 --> 00:13:41.593
out this year, basically.

00:13:41.593 --> 00:13:43.930
The dean of the new school has

00:13:43.930 --> 00:13:46.660
issued a statement last year with

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:49.001
some principles and is doing

00:13:49.001 --> 00:13:51.829
a listening tour and they'll have

00:13:51.829 --> 00:13:54.460
to sort that out at the new school

00:13:54.460 --> 00:13:56.896
over the course of the year how

00:13:56.896 --> 00:13:59.237
they how they want to do that,

00:13:59.237 --> 00:14:01.396
yeah, &gt;&gt; There was [INAUDIBLE] in

00:14:01.396 --> 00:14:03.750
the Wall Street Journal in the last

00:14:03.750 --> 00:14:04.399
week or so

00:14:04.399 --> 00:14:06.674
about decline in applications,

00:14:06.674 --> 00:14:09.110
major business schools, Stanford

00:14:09.110 --> 00:14:11.485
was not mentioned in the article.

00:14:11.485 --> 00:14:13.950
What's the experience here,

00:14:13.950 --> 00:14:16.507
and does that suggest a change in

00:14:16.507 --> 00:14:17.739
the profile for

00:14:17.739 --> 00:14:19.841
people who are applying?

00:14:19.841 --> 00:14:21.240
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so the thing that I

00:14:21.240 --> 00:14:23.179
understand about business school

00:14:23.179 --> 00:14:25.048
application was I think all of you

00:14:25.048 --> 00:14:26.516
know some of us were very,

00:14:26.516 --> 00:14:28.349
very tight to the labor market.

00:14:28.349 --> 00:14:30.329
When the labor market is high,

00:14:30.329 --> 00:14:31.395
business school

00:14:31.395 --> 00:14:33.001
applications are down.

00:14:33.001 --> 00:14:35.233
And when the labor market business

00:14:35.233 --> 00:14:37.158
school applications are up or

00:14:37.158 --> 00:14:38.468
counter cyclical,

00:14:38.468 --> 00:14:39.933
for obvious reasons.

00:14:39.933 --> 00:14:41.051
In last year,

00:14:41.051 --> 00:14:43.849
the labor market was smoking high,

00:14:43.849 --> 00:14:46.467
and our applications were down.

00:14:46.467 --> 00:14:49.489
And I think they were down by

00:14:49.489 --> 00:14:51.070
16% or so.

00:14:51.070 --> 00:14:52.735
In this year the labor markets,

00:14:52.735 --> 00:14:54.398
I guess it's really hard to tell

00:14:54.398 --> 00:14:56.125
depending on the numbers you look

00:14:56.125 --> 00:14:57.482
at, how you look at them,

00:14:57.482 --> 00:14:58.898
there might be very tight or

00:14:58.898 --> 00:15:00.213
it might be cooling off.

00:15:00.213 --> 00:15:02.199
So I guess we're going to find out

00:15:02.199 --> 00:15:04.417
in the next couple of weeks what's

00:15:04.417 --> 00:15:05.114
going on.

00:15:05.114 --> 00:15:07.013
In terms of our class composition,

00:15:07.013 --> 00:15:08.720
the truth is, it hasn't really

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:10.313
mattered our applications.

00:15:10.313 --> 00:15:12.713
I think we're down off our peak,

00:15:12.713 --> 00:15:15.027
which was maybe five years ago or

00:15:15.027 --> 00:15:15.980
something.

00:15:15.980 --> 00:15:17.731
We had our peak year four years ago

00:15:17.731 --> 00:15:19.546
and then we were down a little bit,

00:15:19.546 --> 00:15:20.609
COVID was very weird

00:15:20.609 --> 00:15:21.737
with applications,

00:15:21.737 --> 00:15:23.128
we were down a little bit.

00:15:23.128 --> 00:15:27.111
Other schools were hugely volatile.

00:15:27.111 --> 00:15:30.748
We have two things going for us,

00:15:30.748 --> 00:15:34.384
one is we take a very small number

00:15:34.384 --> 00:15:36.016
of students.

00:15:36.016 --> 00:15:38.850
Almost everybody we admit these

00:15:38.850 --> 00:15:41.370
days comes, about 90% of

00:15:41.370 --> 00:15:44.205
the students we admit come, and

00:15:44.205 --> 00:15:46.940
that's yield is really high.

00:15:46.940 --> 00:15:47.536
And so,

00:15:47.536 --> 00:15:50.264
we're very fortunate in that sense,

00:15:50.264 --> 00:15:52.991
and we've got a class of 2000 that

00:15:52.991 --> 00:15:54.271
was fantastic and

00:15:54.271 --> 00:15:56.500
then we have a class of 420.

00:15:56.500 --> 00:16:00.631
So we're in a pretty good position

00:16:00.631 --> 00:16:02.962
Other schools are much more

00:16:02.962 --> 00:16:05.597
vulnerable to fluctuations in

00:16:05.597 --> 00:16:07.633
the market than we are.

00:16:07.633 --> 00:16:09.440
But I'll just say one more

00:16:09.440 --> 00:16:10.755
thing about that,

00:16:10.755 --> 00:16:12.489
where it shows up for us.

00:16:12.489 --> 00:16:13.722
So there has been a lot of

00:16:13.722 --> 00:16:15.636
discussion about whether there's a

00:16:15.636 --> 00:16:17.363
sort of secular decline in sort of

00:16:17.363 --> 00:16:19.290
two year residential MBA programs.

00:16:19.290 --> 00:16:20.790
And there has been actually,

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:21.991
if you look at numbers.

00:16:21.991 --> 00:16:26.136
Now part of that is that statistic

00:16:26.136 --> 00:16:30.142
is about overall MBA programs.

00:16:30.142 --> 00:16:32.043
And it would be a little bit like

00:16:32.043 --> 00:16:34.018
if you asked Stanford's Dean of

00:16:34.018 --> 00:16:36.140
admissions for undergraduate and

00:16:36.140 --> 00:16:37.823
said college enrollment is

00:16:37.823 --> 00:16:39.152
declining in the US.

00:16:39.152 --> 00:16:41.145
Are you worried that Stanford won't

00:16:41.145 --> 00:16:42.237
have any students in

00:16:42.237 --> 00:16:43.721
the undergraduate class?

00:16:43.721 --> 00:16:45.619
And he'd be like, well, no,

00:16:45.619 --> 00:16:47.012
we had 60,000.

00:16:47.012 --> 00:16:48.945
I don't know how many he had, but

00:16:48.945 --> 00:16:51.086
it's sort of conflating things that

00:16:51.086 --> 00:16:52.813
are a little bit different and

00:16:52.813 --> 00:16:54.264
that's a little bit true

00:16:54.264 --> 00:16:54.969
for us too.

00:16:54.969 --> 00:16:57.871
But it is a reflection of something

00:16:57.871 --> 00:16:59.772
that is affecting us,

00:16:59.772 --> 00:17:02.883
which is career paths have changed.

00:17:02.883 --> 00:17:06.103
And so in fields like finance, in

00:17:06.103 --> 00:17:09.785
consulting, in tech companies, you

00:17:09.785 --> 00:17:12.890
can go far in your career without

00:17:12.890 --> 00:17:15.663
ever stopping to get an MBA.

00:17:15.663 --> 00:17:18.010
So an MBA for many years for

00:17:18.010 --> 00:17:20.573
some people perceived it was

00:17:20.573 --> 00:17:23.467
a necessary rung on the ladder.

00:17:23.467 --> 00:17:24.863
And I don't think anyone thinks of

00:17:24.863 --> 00:17:25.395
it that way.

00:17:25.395 --> 00:17:26.742
Now the good thing is we never

00:17:26.742 --> 00:17:28.201
thought about it that way, and

00:17:28.201 --> 00:17:29.605
I hope none of you ever thought

00:17:29.605 --> 00:17:30.514
about it that way.

00:17:30.514 --> 00:17:31.895
We've always thought of the MBA at

00:17:31.895 --> 00:17:33.133
Stanford is not how do you get up

00:17:33.133 --> 00:17:34.090
one rung on the ladder?

00:17:34.090 --> 00:17:35.887
It's about how do you see every

00:17:35.887 --> 00:17:37.752
possible path you could take and

00:17:37.752 --> 00:17:39.548
get the skills to do any of those

00:17:39.548 --> 00:17:40.112
things.

00:17:40.112 --> 00:17:42.257
So that change in overall MBA,

00:17:42.257 --> 00:17:44.567
the sort of secular changes in MBA

00:17:44.567 --> 00:17:46.877
programs in some ways have played

00:17:46.877 --> 00:17:48.204
to our advantage.

00:17:48.204 --> 00:17:50.389
Because no student comes in

00:17:50.389 --> 00:17:53.049
thinking I need to get this degree

00:17:53.049 --> 00:17:54.762
to get to my next job.

00:17:54.762 --> 00:17:55.542
They can get to

00:17:55.542 --> 00:17:56.909
the next step just fine.

00:17:56.909 --> 00:17:58.939
They come in thinking how is this

00:17:58.939 --> 00:18:00.516
going to set me up for a whole

00:18:00.516 --> 00:18:02.933
career of different opportunities.

00:18:02.933 --> 00:18:04.779
And in some ways that's good,

00:18:04.779 --> 00:18:06.270
that plays to what we were

00:18:06.270 --> 00:18:08.053
doing anyway in our program.

00:18:08.053 --> 00:18:10.285
But we still keep a close eye on

00:18:10.285 --> 00:18:12.339
the year to year admissions

00:18:12.339 --> 00:18:13.690
numbers, yeah?

00:18:18.763 --> 00:18:21.446
&gt;&gt; Thank you for the opportunity.

00:18:21.446 --> 00:18:23.318
You refer to quite a few changes

00:18:23.318 --> 00:18:24.617
in motion globally.

00:18:24.617 --> 00:18:26.321
Can you maybe kind of unpack

00:18:26.321 --> 00:18:28.024
a little bit what are the top

00:18:28.024 --> 00:18:30.247
changes among them that really make

00:18:30.247 --> 00:18:31.730
you think about what's

00:18:31.730 --> 00:18:33.952
the strategy, or how do you deliver

00:18:33.952 --> 00:18:35.750
the ambition of the school?

00:18:35.750 --> 00:18:38.835
&gt;&gt; Yeah, so I'll mention a few.

00:18:38.835 --> 00:18:40.177
I mean, I think, one,

00:18:40.177 --> 00:18:42.191
we already talked about climate and

00:18:42.191 --> 00:18:43.333
sustainability and

00:18:43.333 --> 00:18:45.091
that's a big priority for us.

00:18:45.091 --> 00:18:48.045
And certainly with this incredible

00:18:48.045 --> 00:18:50.802
opportunity with the university

00:18:50.802 --> 00:18:53.559
having a new school to really be at

00:18:53.559 --> 00:18:56.217
the forefront of thinking about

00:18:56.217 --> 00:18:59.072
the response to climate change and

00:18:59.072 --> 00:19:00.853
energy transition.

00:19:00.853 --> 00:19:03.581
And I think that's a great

00:19:03.581 --> 00:19:05.317
opportunity for

00:19:05.317 --> 00:19:09.285
us whether it's entrepreneurship or

00:19:09.285 --> 00:19:13.253
thinking about finance operations,

00:19:13.253 --> 00:19:16.973
different aspects of management.

00:19:16.973 --> 00:19:19.747
The school in Stamford is deeply

00:19:19.747 --> 00:19:22.116
wedded to Silicon Valley.

00:19:22.116 --> 00:19:24.303
And so changes in technology

00:19:24.303 --> 00:19:26.218
particularly in digital

00:19:26.218 --> 00:19:29.143
technologies, they come from here.

00:19:29.143 --> 00:19:30.601
We are the source of so

00:19:30.601 --> 00:19:33.115
much of that innovation and change.

00:19:33.115 --> 00:19:34.843
And to my mind, one of the big

00:19:34.843 --> 00:19:36.788
things that has happened here,

00:19:36.788 --> 00:19:37.580
one is just,

00:19:37.580 --> 00:19:39.670
there's been such a revolution in

00:19:39.670 --> 00:19:41.759
areas like machine learning, and

00:19:41.759 --> 00:19:44.137
artificial intelligence, and data,

00:19:44.137 --> 00:19:45.232
and algorithms.

00:19:45.232 --> 00:19:47.911
And that's a great opportunity for

00:19:47.911 --> 00:19:49.787
our students because the

00:19:49.787 --> 00:19:52.377
combination of the technological

00:19:52.377 --> 00:19:54.610
change and the business model

00:19:54.610 --> 00:19:57.199
changes that intersection is such

00:19:57.199 --> 00:19:58.997
a powerful place to be.

00:19:58.997 --> 00:20:02.825
And it's a place where our students

00:20:02.825 --> 00:20:06.275
have the opportunity to get to.

00:20:06.275 --> 00:20:07.325
When I came to Stanford as

00:20:07.325 --> 00:20:08.825
a faculty member 20 years ago and I

00:20:08.825 --> 00:20:10.425
worked on the economics technology,

00:20:10.425 --> 00:20:11.475
it felt in Silicon Valley

00:20:11.475 --> 00:20:12.875
like we were really divorced from

00:20:12.875 --> 00:20:13.376
the world.

00:20:13.376 --> 00:20:14.057
We're just creating

00:20:14.057 --> 00:20:14.743
these great things.

00:20:14.743 --> 00:20:17.049
There's a lot of bad stuff going

00:20:17.049 --> 00:20:18.420
on, but not here.

00:20:18.420 --> 00:20:20.165
Here's where we just create these

00:20:20.165 --> 00:20:21.550
amazing products that make

00:20:21.550 --> 00:20:23.001
everyone's lives better.

00:20:23.001 --> 00:20:24.390
Everyone here has a more

00:20:24.390 --> 00:20:26.198
sophisticated complex view of

00:20:26.198 --> 00:20:27.240
the world now, or

00:20:27.240 --> 00:20:28.570
at least I think we do.

00:20:28.570 --> 00:20:30.755
Or at least we need to have one.

00:20:30.755 --> 00:20:32.706
So that's a really important

00:20:32.706 --> 00:20:33.568
area for us.

00:20:33.568 --> 00:20:35.571
And it's an important area for

00:20:35.571 --> 00:20:37.882
the whole university to be thinking

00:20:37.882 --> 00:20:39.500
about the potential from

00:20:39.500 --> 00:20:40.656
technology, but

00:20:40.656 --> 00:20:42.890
also the complexities of how do you

00:20:42.890 --> 00:20:45.289
guide the direction of technology?

00:20:45.289 --> 00:20:47.532
What are the second order effects,

00:20:47.532 --> 00:20:48.438
and so forth.

00:20:48.438 --> 00:20:49.333
So just to pick two,

00:20:49.333 --> 00:20:50.229
those would be, and

00:20:50.229 --> 00:20:51.653
Nat's going to talk about a sort of

00:20:51.653 --> 00:20:53.313
another broader set of governance.

00:20:53.313 --> 00:20:55.833
But I'll let her do the talking on

00:20:55.833 --> 00:20:56.644
that one.

00:20:56.644 --> 00:20:59.265
But if I had to pick two or three,

00:20:59.265 --> 00:21:01.022
those would be them.

00:21:01.022 --> 00:21:02.601
Okay, in the front.

00:21:02.601 --> 00:21:09.734
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] &gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:21:09.734 --> 00:21:11.521
&gt;&gt; Couple of years in the way

00:21:11.521 --> 00:21:12.969
teaching was done,

00:21:12.969 --> 00:21:15.354
in the way students interacted,

00:21:15.354 --> 00:21:17.397
in all the aspects of how that

00:21:17.397 --> 00:21:20.229
happened, and now you've come back.

00:21:20.229 --> 00:21:21.377
What do you see as

00:21:21.377 --> 00:21:23.428
the key takeaways from that in

00:21:23.428 --> 00:21:25.156
terms of going forward?

00:21:25.156 --> 00:21:26.633
What did we learn?

00:21:26.633 --> 00:21:27.573
Just as you said,

00:21:27.573 --> 00:21:29.393
we're changing the way we work and

00:21:29.393 --> 00:21:31.289
rethinking the last 100 years.

00:21:31.289 --> 00:21:32.710
Is that happening in

00:21:32.710 --> 00:21:34.973
the education process and how?

00:21:34.973 --> 00:21:35.637
&gt;&gt; Yeah,

00:21:35.637 --> 00:21:38.293
okay, that is such a great,

00:21:38.293 --> 00:21:39.965
great question.

00:21:39.965 --> 00:21:41.899
So during COVID, I read that there

00:21:41.899 --> 00:21:43.300
was a university group,

00:21:43.300 --> 00:21:45.109
the long range recovery group.

00:21:45.109 --> 00:21:47.082
And I led that group with a faculty

00:21:47.082 --> 00:21:48.632
member from the H&amp;S, and

00:21:48.632 --> 00:21:50.183
that was the question that

00:21:50.183 --> 00:21:52.227
was asked of this group, and we had

00:21:52.227 --> 00:21:54.432
people from across the university.

00:21:54.432 --> 00:21:57.907
And we basically came back and

00:21:57.907 --> 00:22:00.826
said, we had two answers

00:22:00.826 --> 00:22:03.052
to this question.

00:22:03.052 --> 00:22:05.522
One of the answers was, this is

00:22:05.522 --> 00:22:08.280
a time of incredible innovation.

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:10.432
It really did force everybody to

00:22:10.432 --> 00:22:12.186
think about how to teach,

00:22:12.186 --> 00:22:14.019
how to use classroom time,

00:22:14.019 --> 00:22:15.623
how to use technology.

00:22:15.623 --> 00:22:16.988
I mean, it's a revelation what you

00:22:16.988 --> 00:22:17.950
can do with technology.

00:22:17.950 --> 00:22:19.803
Who knew that on Friday night,

00:22:19.803 --> 00:22:21.800
the provost could send an email at

00:22:21.800 --> 00:22:22.941
Stanford and say,

00:22:22.941 --> 00:22:24.153
on Monday morning,

00:22:24.153 --> 00:22:25.881
we will be teaching online.

00:22:25.881 --> 00:22:27.741
And then on Monday morning,

00:22:27.741 --> 00:22:29.450
we were teaching online.

00:22:29.450 --> 00:22:30.788
The whole school was online.

00:22:30.788 --> 00:22:32.439
We did it over a weekend.

00:22:32.439 --> 00:22:34.169
Of course we were terrible at it

00:22:34.169 --> 00:22:36.172
when we started, I mean truly bad.

00:22:36.172 --> 00:22:38.127
The first day of COVID after we

00:22:38.127 --> 00:22:40.318
went online, I was giving a guest

00:22:40.318 --> 00:22:42.361
lecture in one of the classes.

00:22:42.361 --> 00:22:43.321
It's a class taught by

00:22:43.321 --> 00:22:44.741
Peter Wendell and Eric Schmidt.

00:22:44.741 --> 00:22:46.440
And I was their guest, and

00:22:46.440 --> 00:22:47.985
we get into the class and

00:22:47.985 --> 00:22:49.384
I welcome the class.

00:22:49.384 --> 00:22:50.752
And I say, welcome to

00:22:50.752 --> 00:22:52.348
the GSB online on Zoom and

00:22:52.348 --> 00:22:54.704
they're waving at me and I'm like,

00:22:54.704 --> 00:22:56.376
why are they waving at me?

00:22:56.376 --> 00:23:00.458
And they say the camera is that

00:23:00.458 --> 00:23:01.277
way.

00:23:01.277 --> 00:23:06.574
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; So

00:23:06.574 --> 00:23:09.525
we got better over 18 months.

00:23:09.525 --> 00:23:10.253
[LAUGH] And

00:23:10.253 --> 00:23:12.892
some of that's coming back I think

00:23:12.892 --> 00:23:14.803
into the MBA classroom,

00:23:14.803 --> 00:23:17.442
people are using more Zoom to bring

00:23:17.442 --> 00:23:18.898
speakers in more.

00:23:18.898 --> 00:23:20.058
They did lots of video,

00:23:20.058 --> 00:23:21.914
people made videos, they used that,

00:23:21.914 --> 00:23:23.249
more flipped classrooms,

00:23:23.249 --> 00:23:24.072
types of stuff.

00:23:24.072 --> 00:23:26.513
The place where we're really

00:23:26.513 --> 00:23:28.660
using it actually is in our

00:23:28.660 --> 00:23:31.101
shorter flexible programs,

00:23:31.101 --> 00:23:32.965
executive programs.

00:23:32.965 --> 00:23:33.769
We had to stop all

00:23:33.769 --> 00:23:35.556
our executive programs basically.

00:23:35.556 --> 00:23:36.266
We have a big executive education

00:23:36.266 --> 00:23:38.199
business. It generates about $80

00:23:38.199 --> 00:23:39.955
million a year in revenue.

00:23:39.955 --> 00:23:41.619
90% of it was on campus summer

00:23:41.619 --> 00:23:42.240
programs.

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:44.922
It went to zero during COVID for

00:23:44.922 --> 00:23:46.166
two summers.

00:23:46.166 --> 00:23:48.612
And instead we basically spent 18

00:23:48.612 --> 00:23:51.064
months just trying out new things.

00:23:51.064 --> 00:23:52.498
And some of them were not

00:23:52.498 --> 00:23:53.432
successful and

00:23:53.432 --> 00:23:55.093
some of them were amazing.

00:23:55.093 --> 00:23:55.857
So I'll just give you

00:23:55.857 --> 00:23:56.582
a couple examples.

00:23:56.582 --> 00:23:59.264
So one is We started this type of

00:23:59.264 --> 00:24:01.793
program in education where these

00:24:01.793 --> 00:24:04.232
are just two or three-day short

00:24:04.232 --> 00:24:06.129
intensive refreshers on

00:24:06.129 --> 00:24:08.748
a particular subject that faculty

00:24:08.748 --> 00:24:10.571
are keen to talk about.

00:24:10.571 --> 00:24:12.595
Turns out it's very hard to fill

00:24:12.595 --> 00:24:14.695
for two days to talk about Fintech

00:24:14.695 --> 00:24:16.495
or talk about AI or talk about

00:24:16.495 --> 00:24:18.820
bitcoiners or something, wherever

00:24:18.820 --> 00:24:20.851
the faculty are interested in.

00:24:20.851 --> 00:24:22.359
Getting a global audience

00:24:22.359 --> 00:24:24.141
to spend two days on Zoom kind of

00:24:24.141 --> 00:24:25.649
just diving into a topic of

00:24:25.649 --> 00:24:27.857
excitement to faculty, no problem.

00:24:27.857 --> 00:24:29.497
So this year we're actually we're

00:24:29.497 --> 00:24:31.085
going to work with the engineering

00:24:31.085 --> 00:24:32.561
school and we're going to set up

00:24:32.561 --> 00:24:33.217
a platform for

00:24:33.217 --> 00:24:34.749
faculty across Stanford to offer

00:24:34.749 --> 00:24:36.027
the Stanford intensives.

00:24:36.027 --> 00:24:37.420
We could have done that before

00:24:37.420 --> 00:24:38.982
COVID but we probably would never

00:24:38.982 --> 00:24:40.377
have come up with something,

00:24:40.377 --> 00:24:41.994
like we wouldn't have bothered or

00:24:41.994 --> 00:24:43.641
just wouldn't have come to mind.

00:24:43.641 --> 00:24:45.610
And so now some things like that.

00:24:45.610 --> 00:24:47.342
So that's been one great source of

00:24:47.342 --> 00:24:48.065
innovation.

00:24:48.065 --> 00:24:49.919
Okay, that was the first lesson

00:24:49.919 --> 00:24:51.231
kind of a revelation.

00:24:51.231 --> 00:24:53.399
The second revelation was what I

00:24:53.399 --> 00:24:54.443
started with,

00:24:54.443 --> 00:24:56.770
which is MBA education is just much

00:24:56.770 --> 00:24:58.067
better in person.

00:24:58.067 --> 00:24:59.633
I mean, it is just like people

00:24:59.633 --> 00:25:00.962
were talking for COVID.

00:25:00.962 --> 00:25:03.103
Maybe the MBA will be a hybrid

00:25:03.103 --> 00:25:05.335
degree people want to Zoom in,

00:25:05.335 --> 00:25:07.742
turns out, no, our students do

00:25:07.742 --> 00:25:09.183
not want to Zoom in.

00:25:09.183 --> 00:25:11.726
They spent the whole COVID in

00:25:11.726 --> 00:25:14.588
the residences chafing against

00:25:14.588 --> 00:25:16.928
the COVID restrictions.

00:25:16.928 --> 00:25:18.193
And when they came off,

00:25:18.193 --> 00:25:19.837
you just see that that is like,

00:25:19.837 --> 00:25:21.482
it's all about interacting,

00:25:21.482 --> 00:25:23.458
it's all about the people you meet.

00:25:23.458 --> 00:25:24.833
And the faculty and

00:25:24.833 --> 00:25:26.985
engaging in the classroom and

00:25:26.985 --> 00:25:28.275
mixing it up, and

00:25:28.275 --> 00:25:30.856
it's an affirmation of the value of

00:25:30.856 --> 00:25:33.278
what happens here on the campus.

00:25:33.278 --> 00:25:35.267
And part of the lesson of COVID is

00:25:35.267 --> 00:25:36.741
we just gotta get back to

00:25:36.741 --> 00:25:37.561
doing that.

00:25:37.561 --> 00:25:40.282
Get people back together.

00:25:40.282 --> 00:25:40.992
My gosh, okay,

00:25:40.992 --> 00:25:41.955
how about in the back,

00:25:41.955 --> 00:25:43.334
you had your hand up for a while.

00:25:43.334 --> 00:25:47.407
&gt;&gt; [INAUDIBLE] Here, the flip side

00:25:47.407 --> 00:25:50.388
of that are the risks.

00:25:50.388 --> 00:25:51.725
What are there two or

00:25:51.725 --> 00:25:53.616
three risks that are biggest

00:25:53.616 --> 00:25:55.514
in your mind about all this?

00:25:55.514 --> 00:26:01.839
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean there's,

00:26:01.839 --> 00:26:09.375
gosh there's always risks.

00:26:09.375 --> 00:26:10.624
When I think about sort of

00:26:10.624 --> 00:26:11.934
the things that worry me,

00:26:11.934 --> 00:26:13.301
a lot of things that worry me

00:26:13.301 --> 00:26:14.861
are just about sort of people.

00:26:14.861 --> 00:26:17.239
The people on the campus you're

00:26:17.239 --> 00:26:19.442
responsible for people and so

00:26:19.442 --> 00:26:21.028
during COVID that was

00:26:21.028 --> 00:26:23.056
certainly something that I

00:26:23.056 --> 00:26:24.656
worried a lot about.

00:26:24.656 --> 00:26:28.405
And even now, I worry about issues

00:26:28.405 --> 00:26:31.768
like student mental health and

00:26:31.768 --> 00:26:34.756
issues like our students.

00:26:34.756 --> 00:26:37.353
And our faculty and folks that they

00:26:37.353 --> 00:26:39.412
came through a hard time and

00:26:39.412 --> 00:26:42.020
it's still pretty challenging.

00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:45.011
Institutionally I think in some

00:26:45.011 --> 00:26:48.335
sense it's Stanford, the biggest

00:26:48.335 --> 00:26:50.993
risks at Stanford are that we

00:26:50.993 --> 00:26:54.114
become, this is going to.

00:26:54.114 --> 00:26:55.043
I don't want to say something

00:26:55.043 --> 00:26:55.934
that's going to sound bad for

00:26:55.934 --> 00:26:56.561
our peer schools.

00:26:56.561 --> 00:26:59.052
But the best thing about Stanford,

00:26:59.052 --> 00:27:01.294
the thing that brought me here,

00:27:01.294 --> 00:27:03.703
particularly it brought me here as

00:27:03.703 --> 00:27:05.046
a faculty member.

00:27:05.046 --> 00:27:07.432
And kept me here on the faculty for

00:27:07.432 --> 00:27:10.162
20 years instead of going somewhere

00:27:10.162 --> 00:27:11.952
else is that Stanford is a

00:27:11.952 --> 00:27:14.955
relentlessly forward-looking place.

00:27:14.955 --> 00:27:16.702
Stanford is very unusual in higher

00:27:16.702 --> 00:27:17.370
education.

00:27:17.370 --> 00:27:18.865
If you think about it,

00:27:18.865 --> 00:27:20.362
higher education is,

00:27:20.362 --> 00:27:22.499
how many industries are there?

00:27:22.499 --> 00:27:26.721
We're the top three organizations

00:27:26.721 --> 00:27:28.410
in 1705,

00:27:28.410 --> 00:27:32.217
are still in the top five today.

00:27:32.217 --> 00:27:34.233
Higher education is like that.

00:27:34.233 --> 00:27:36.914
And what's amazing about Stanford

00:27:36.914 --> 00:27:38.521
is just 7 years ago,

00:27:38.521 --> 00:27:41.389
Stanford was a regional university.

00:27:41.389 --> 00:27:43.360
And it just has had this incredible

00:27:43.360 --> 00:27:45.201
ascent over many, many years.

00:27:45.201 --> 00:27:47.369
Partly because of the people like

00:27:47.369 --> 00:27:49.614
you who went through the school and

00:27:49.614 --> 00:27:51.551
have gone on an added luster to

00:27:51.551 --> 00:27:53.959
the reputation of the institution.

00:27:53.959 --> 00:27:55.193
And because the faculty and

00:27:55.193 --> 00:27:57.147
the current students and all that.

00:27:57.147 --> 00:27:58.995
And what has driven that is

00:27:58.995 --> 00:28:01.519
that it's a very forward-looking

00:28:01.519 --> 00:28:03.961
entrepreneurial, innovative,

00:28:03.961 --> 00:28:05.990
not afraid of change place.

00:28:05.990 --> 00:28:07.750
And so the biggest risk to us is

00:28:07.750 --> 00:28:09.652
that somehow we would rest on our

00:28:09.652 --> 00:28:11.776
laurels or we would be complacent.

00:28:11.776 --> 00:28:13.972
Or we would start spending too much

00:28:13.972 --> 00:28:16.017
time sort of tried to protect the

00:28:16.017 --> 00:28:18.139
things we have instead of keeping

00:28:18.139 --> 00:28:20.414
the drive and the forward-looking

00:28:20.414 --> 00:28:22.613
and that opportunity mindset that

00:28:22.613 --> 00:28:24.694
has made this place so special.

00:28:24.694 --> 00:28:26.045
&gt;&gt; Remember the recruiting rush of

00:28:26.045 --> 00:28:27.546
the summer with the shrimp cocktail

00:28:27.546 --> 00:28:28.947
and the buffets, and especially

00:28:28.947 --> 00:28:30.509
the second-year recruiting rush.

00:28:30.509 --> 00:28:32.059
&gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; You talked about how

00:28:32.059 --> 00:28:33.719
people are rethinking work.

00:28:33.719 --> 00:28:34.907
&gt;&gt; Yeah. &gt;&gt; How has recruiting

00:28:34.907 --> 00:28:36.213
changed from what it was then

00:28:36.213 --> 00:28:37.245
&gt;&gt; That is such a great question.

00:28:37.245 --> 00:28:38.338
How is recruiting change?

00:28:38.338 --> 00:28:39.573
That is such a great question.

00:28:39.573 --> 00:28:40.917
So here's the interesting thing

00:28:40.917 --> 00:28:41.545
about the GSB.

00:28:41.545 --> 00:28:42.963
This is in recruiting.

00:28:42.963 --> 00:28:45.341
This is true even before COVID.

00:28:45.341 --> 00:28:47.297
Basically, all of our peer schools,

00:28:47.297 --> 00:28:48.948
two-thirds of the students get

00:28:48.948 --> 00:28:50.539
their jobs through on-campus

00:28:50.539 --> 00:28:51.456
recruiting, and

00:28:51.456 --> 00:28:53.307
through their summer internship.

00:28:53.307 --> 00:28:55.430
We were in a situation before COVID

00:28:55.430 --> 00:28:57.482
where 10% of our students were

00:28:57.482 --> 00:28:59.681
taking their summer internship and

00:28:59.681 --> 00:29:01.233
turning that into a job.

00:29:01.233 --> 00:29:03.049
And only about a third of our

00:29:03.049 --> 00:29:05.419
students or less were getting their

00:29:05.419 --> 00:29:08.036
jobs through on-campus recruiting.

00:29:08.036 --> 00:29:10.678
Instead, we had moved already

00:29:10.678 --> 00:29:13.726
before COVID, basically to a world

00:29:13.726 --> 00:29:16.379
of kind of network job search.

00:29:16.379 --> 00:29:18.566
Where our students were instead of

00:29:18.566 --> 00:29:20.754
kind of waiting for the recruiters

00:29:20.754 --> 00:29:22.944
to come to them, we're basically

00:29:22.944 --> 00:29:25.132
looking around through alumni who

00:29:25.132 --> 00:29:26.504
was a huge resource.

00:29:26.504 --> 00:29:27.258
Thank you for

00:29:27.258 --> 00:29:28.978
returning people's calls.

00:29:28.978 --> 00:29:30.194
I'm going to come back to that in

00:29:30.194 --> 00:29:31.508
a second actually, because you're

00:29:31.508 --> 00:29:32.833
so important actually in this.

00:29:32.833 --> 00:29:34.717
Or they were looking at LinkedIn or

00:29:34.717 --> 00:29:35.692
certain times and

00:29:35.692 --> 00:29:36.667
then going out and

00:29:36.667 --> 00:29:38.684
proactively trying to find places.

00:29:38.684 --> 00:29:40.130
And some of them still go through

00:29:40.130 --> 00:29:41.740
recruiting for consulting firms or

00:29:41.740 --> 00:29:43.250
private equity or what have you.

00:29:43.250 --> 00:29:46.188
But a lot of them were already in

00:29:46.188 --> 00:29:48.449
this kind of new world of

00:29:48.449 --> 00:29:51.627
decentralized network search.

00:29:51.627 --> 00:29:52.791
We had to go through a huge

00:29:52.791 --> 00:29:54.290
transition in the way we support

00:29:54.290 --> 00:29:55.845
careers in our career management

00:29:55.845 --> 00:29:57.232
center over the last couple of

00:29:57.232 --> 00:29:58.362
years to support that.

00:29:58.362 --> 00:30:00.337
And COVID, I think just sort of

00:30:00.337 --> 00:30:02.618
a little bit excited that, I mean,

00:30:02.618 --> 00:30:04.669
recruiting now has moved to Zoom

00:30:04.669 --> 00:30:05.667
in large part.

00:30:05.667 --> 00:30:07.299
At least the first rounds of

00:30:07.299 --> 00:30:09.288
interviews and so forth, but our

00:30:09.288 --> 00:30:11.276
students were kind of ahead of the

00:30:11.276 --> 00:30:13.204
curve in this as they often are.

00:30:13.204 --> 00:30:15.250
The last thing I'll just say about

00:30:15.250 --> 00:30:17.297
this with about the role you play,

00:30:17.297 --> 00:30:18.919
you play a big role in our job

00:30:18.919 --> 00:30:19.776
placements.

00:30:19.776 --> 00:30:22.213
And I'd say two things about it,

00:30:22.213 --> 00:30:24.300
one is just to come back to the

00:30:24.300 --> 00:30:26.926
theme of gratitude I started with.

00:30:26.926 --> 00:30:29.207
During COVID when we started the

00:30:29.207 --> 00:30:31.826
pandemic, we had a catastrophe with

00:30:31.826 --> 00:30:34.192
the student job searches because

00:30:34.192 --> 00:30:36.642
the internships got cancelled and

00:30:36.642 --> 00:30:38.434
the job market dried up.

00:30:38.434 --> 00:30:41.257
And when we were in March of 2020,

00:30:41.257 --> 00:30:43.551
our Career Centre came to me and

00:30:43.551 --> 00:30:45.403
to the rest of the folks in

00:30:45.403 --> 00:30:47.272
the school leadership.

00:30:47.272 --> 00:30:49.098
And said we don't have enough jobs

00:30:49.098 --> 00:30:50.925
just posted at the Career Center to

00:30:50.925 --> 00:30:52.191
get our students jobs.

00:30:52.191 --> 00:30:54.710
We were about 150 jobs short, and

00:30:54.710 --> 00:30:56.182
internships short.

00:30:56.182 --> 00:30:57.588
And a time I wrote out to all of

00:30:57.588 --> 00:30:58.998
you and to the other alumni,

00:30:58.998 --> 00:31:00.193
they said we need help.

00:31:00.193 --> 00:31:01.103
And actually,

00:31:01.103 --> 00:31:02.922
that was one of the most amazing

00:31:02.922 --> 00:31:05.022
responses that I've ever seen from

00:31:05.022 --> 00:31:06.428
an alumni population.

00:31:06.428 --> 00:31:08.876
The GSB alumni credit 300 jobs and

00:31:08.876 --> 00:31:11.248
internships in about three weeks.

00:31:11.248 --> 00:31:13.146
And all of our students got great

00:31:13.146 --> 00:31:14.207
opportunities.

00:31:14.207 --> 00:31:15.934
That was incredible.

00:31:15.934 --> 00:31:18.639
I mean, seriously incredible.

00:31:18.639 --> 00:31:20.783
And one of my colleagues who

00:31:20.783 --> 00:31:23.302
teaches here has taught here for

00:31:23.302 --> 00:31:24.421
a long time and

00:31:24.421 --> 00:31:26.751
is an alum of another school on

00:31:26.751 --> 00:31:29.083
the East Coast that sometimes

00:31:29.083 --> 00:31:31.712
people think of as a pure school.

00:31:31.712 --> 00:31:34.715
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; I hope I'm not being

00:31:34.715 --> 00:31:35.247
recorded.

00:31:35.247 --> 00:31:39.012
No, [LAUGH] I am being recorded.

00:31:39.012 --> 00:31:39.938
But I'll tell the story anyway

00:31:39.938 --> 00:31:40.658
because it's a good story.

00:31:40.658 --> 00:31:44.222
So he said, he was and he said,

00:31:44.222 --> 00:31:47.245
at my alma mater the alumni book is

00:31:47.245 --> 00:31:50.603
really thick, it's hugely thick.

00:31:50.603 --> 00:31:52.552
He said, any industry you look

00:31:52.552 --> 00:31:54.354
in there's tons of people.

00:31:54.354 --> 00:31:56.961
But no one calls you back.

00:31:56.961 --> 00:31:59.640
The GSB books its kind of thin and

00:31:59.640 --> 00:32:02.419
thinner, pretty thick but not as

00:32:02.419 --> 00:32:05.408
thick but everyone calls you back.

00:32:05.408 --> 00:32:06.293
And that's amazing and

00:32:06.293 --> 00:32:07.355
that's such a huge asset for

00:32:07.355 --> 00:32:07.938
our students.

00:32:07.938 --> 00:32:09.188
I just told our students today

00:32:09.188 --> 00:32:10.289
because they were asking about

00:32:10.289 --> 00:32:11.691
what's going to happen to the labor

00:32:11.691 --> 00:32:12.506
market this year.

00:32:12.506 --> 00:32:14.393
That's a real ace in the hole

00:32:14.393 --> 00:32:15.105
for them.

00:32:15.105 --> 00:32:16.621
So you all are the ones who make

00:32:16.621 --> 00:32:17.357
that happen.

00:32:17.357 --> 00:32:19.249
So thank you for everything you do.

00:32:19.249 --> 00:32:20.697
And thank you for everything you

00:32:20.697 --> 00:32:22.259
do more generally for the school.

00:32:22.259 --> 00:32:23.792
We're so lucky to have

00:32:23.792 --> 00:32:25.574
this alumni population.

00:32:25.574 --> 00:32:26.918
And it's so great to

00:32:26.918 --> 00:32:28.736
get to see you here today and

00:32:28.736 --> 00:32:30.883
I hope you have a great weekend.

00:32:30.883 --> 00:32:39.035
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:32:39.035 --> 00:32:44.107
[MUSIC]

